---
import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import { SITE_DESCRIPTION, SITE_TITLE } from "../consts";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
    <head>
        <BaseHead title={SITE_TITLE} description={SITE_DESCRIPTION} />
    </head>

    <body>
        <Header />

        <main>
            <!-- Hero -->
            <section class="hero">
                <h1>I TURN COMPLEXITY INTO STRUCTURE</h1>

                <p class="hero-description">
                    Complex systems rarely fail because they're too complex.
                    They fail when nobody understands <strong
                        >what actually matters.</strong
                    >
                </p>

                <p class="hero-description">
                    I help engineering teams make sense of difficult systems,
                    make hard technical decisions, and build software that holds
                    up in the real world.
                </p>

                <a href="#problem">&darr; KEEP READING</a>
            </section>

            <!-- Problem -->
            <section class="problem" id="problem">
                <h2>WHEN EVERYTHING IS CONNECTED, NOTHING IS OBVIOUS</h2>

                <div>
                    <p>
                        Requirements. Constraints. Legacy decisions. Distributed
                        systems. Real-time data. Hardware. Protocols.
                        Performance.
                    </p>

                    <p>Everything affects everything else.</p>

                    <p>And that's where things usually go wrong.</p>

                    <p>
                        <strong
                            >The hardest engineering problems aren't solved by
                            writing more code.</strong
                        >
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        They're solved by understanding the system well enough
                        to know <strong
                            >what code should exist in the first place.</strong
                        >
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Services -->
            <section class="services">
                <h2>
                    I HELP TURN TECHNICAL UNCERTAINTY INTO SOMETHING YOU CAN
                    BUILD
                </h2>

                <div class="service-grid">
                    <article class="service-card">
                        <h3>Architecture &amp; System Design</h3>

                        <p>
                            You have requirements, constraints and a vague idea
                            of where you're going. I turn that into a system
                            with clear boundaries, explicit trade-offs and
                            decisions you can actually build on.
                        </p>
                    </article>

                    <article class="service-card">
                        <h3>Systems Diagnosis</h3>

                        <p>
                            Something works. But nobody really knows why. I dig
                            into difficult existing systems, find the real
                            constraints and separate symptoms from the problems
                            underneath them.
                        </p>
                    </article>

                    <article class="service-card">
                        <h3>Engineering Rescue</h3>

                        <p>
                            The system has become painful. Changes are risky.
                            Performance is unpredictable. Nobody wants to touch
                            certain parts anymore. I help make it understandable
                            again — and give the team a way forward.
                        </p>
                    </article>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Process -->
            <section class="process">
                <h2>I DON'T START WITH TECHNOLOGY</h2>

                <div>
                    <div class="process-flow">
                        <p class="process-step">Ambiguity</p>
                        <p class="process-step">Understanding</p>
                        <p class="process-step">Structure</p>
                        <p class="process-step">Decisions</p>
                        <p class="process-step">Engineering</p>
                        <p class="process-step">Reliable System</p>
                    </div>

                    <p>Because choosing a technology is easy.</p>

                    <p>
                        <strong
                            >Knowing what the system actually needs is the hard
                            part.</strong
                        >
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Selected Work -->
            <section class="selected-work" id="work">
                <h2>REAL SYSTEMS. REAL CONSTRAINTS</h2>

                <p>
                    I don't believe architecture should be discussed in a
                    vacuum. These are some of the systems I've actually built
                    and evolved.
                </p>

                <div class="project-grid">
                    <article class="project-card">
                        <h3>AKVAKT</h3>

                        <p class="project-lead">
                            <strong>Designing a platform from zero.</strong>
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            Industrial edge systems running at customer sites.
                            Real-time data. Constrained hardware. Industrial
                            protocols. Remote operation.
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            A platform had to emerge from all of those
                            constraints — without turning every new customer
                            into a new codebase.
                        </p>

                        <a href="/notebook/akvakt">Read the story &rarr;</a>
                    </article>

                    <article class="project-card">
                        <h3>CIXOR</h3>

                        <p class="project-lead">
                            <strong
                                >Making a difficult system incremental.</strong
                            >
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            Large workloads. Distributed processing. Operational
                            complexity.
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            The challenge wasn't simply making it faster. It was
                            changing how the system processed work without
                            breaking everything around it.
                        </p>

                        <a href="/notebook/cixor">Read the story &rarr;</a>
                    </article>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Engineering Notebook -->
            <section class="notebook">
                <h2>I WRITE DOWN THE DECISIONS BEHIND THE SYSTEMS</h2>

                <p>
                    Not tutorials. Not lists of technologies. Not “10 things I
                    learned this week”.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Technical stories about real systems — what made them
                    difficult, what decisions were made, what went wrong, and
                    why the architecture ended up the way it did.
                </p>

                <a href="/notebook">Explore the Engineering Notebook &rarr;</a>
            </section>

            <!-- About -->
            <section class="about">
                <h2>ABOUT</h2>

                <h3>José María Sánchez Salas</h3>

                <p><strong>Software Engineer &amp; Architect</strong></p>

                <p>
                    Around eight years building and evolving complex systems —
                    from backend platforms to industrial edge environments.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I tend to end up where the system has become difficult to
                    understand.
                </p>

                <p>That's usually where the interesting engineering starts.</p>

                <a href="/about">More about me &rarr;</a>
            </section>

            <!-- Contact -->
            <section class="contact" id="contact">
                <h2>HAVE A DIFFICULT SYSTEM?</h2>

                <p>
                    Good. That's usually more interesting than starting from
                    scratch.
                </p>

                <p>
                    If your system is hard to understand, your architecture
                    needs direction, or an engineering problem has become
                    difficult enough that someone needs to take ownership:
                </p>

                <p><strong>Let's talk.</strong></p>

                <a href="mailto:josemaria@jmssalas.com">Send me an email &rarr;</a>
            </section>
        </main>

        <Footer />
    </body>
</html>
